"""Build the local project page and portfolio entry from saved data and exports."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,html
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];P=R/'site';P.mkdir(exist_ok=True);base='https://github.com/Flex-commits/30-days-mapping/tree/main/day03-africa-to-scale';rows=json.loads((R/'data/processed/continents/asia-country-coverage.json').read_text());body=''.join('<tr><td>'+html.escape(x['country'])+('</td><td>Asian portion' if x['id'] in ['RUS','TUR','KAZ','GEO','AZE'] else '</td><td>Source country/territory')+f"</td><td>{x['covered_km2']:,.0f}</td><td>{x['covered_percent']:.1f}%</td></tr>" for x in rows)
page='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Africa, to Scale — Thomas Hagos</title><meta name="description" content="Six continent comparisons at one preserved area scale. Day 03 / Polygons by Thomas Hagos."><style>:root{color-scheme:dark}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#101e2b;color:#f2eee2;font:17px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1120px;margin:auto;padding:55px 28px}h1{font-size:clamp(42px,7vw,80px);line-height:1.08;margin:16px 0}h2{font-size:30px;line-height:1.2;margin:65px 0 20px}a{color:#ffbf69;text-underline-offset:4px}p{max-width:820px}.eyebrow{color:#ffbf69;letter-spacing:.12em;font-size:12px}.lead{font-size:23px;color:#a8b7c2}.links{display:flex;gap:20px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:25px 0}img{max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block}figure{margin:35px 0}figcaption,small{color:#a8b7c2;font-size:14px}.numbers{display:flex;gap:50px;flex-wrap:wrap;border-top:1px solid #405562;border-bottom:1px solid #405562;padding:25px 0;margin:35px 0}.numbers b{font-size:32px;display:block;color:#ffbf69}.table{overflow:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px}th,td{text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #344a58;padding:10px}footer{margin-top:70px;border-top:1px solid #344a58;padding-top:25px;color:#a8b7c2}@media(max-width:600px){main{padding:35px 18px}.lead{font-size:19px}.numbers{gap:24px}h2{font-size:25px}}</style><main><div class="eyebrow">THOMAS HAGOS · 2026 MUSA 30-DAY MAP CHALLENGE · DAY 03 / POLYGONS</div><h1>Africa, to Scale</h1><p class="lead">Continent outlines compared at one consistent area scale.</p><p>Africa is the reference in five comparisons. In the sixth, the perspective reverses: its outline crosses a country-colored Asia. The shapes move and rotate, but their calculated areas stay the same.</p><nav class="links"><a href="../outputs/africa-to-scale.png">Download the full map</a><a href="../outputs/03-thomas-hagos.png">Gallery image</a><a href="REPO">Code and sources</a></nav><div class="numbers"><div><b>29.90m km²</b>Africa’s calculated surface area</div><div><b>59.2%</b>South America / Africa</div><div><b>67.9%</b>Africa / Asia</div></div><figure><a href="../outputs/africa-to-scale.png"><img src="../outputs/africa-to-scale-preview.png" alt="Six equal-area continent overlays: South America, Australia, Antarctica, Europe and North America over Africa; Africa over Asia. Hatching identifies portions beyond each host."></a><figcaption>Main panels share the same physical area scale. Smaller-scale island context insets retain the full original extent; dashed boxes identify the main views. The cream outline identifies Africa; hatching marks portions beyond the host outline.</figcaption></figure><h2>For the small screen</h2><p>Six individual panels lead with each area comparison. Each panel uses one area scale within its overlay; view scales vary across slides. Full-extent island context is retained in the labeled inset.</p><nav class="links"><a href="../outputs/linkedin/01-south-america.png">1 · South America</a><a href="../outputs/linkedin/02-australia.png">2 · Australia</a><a href="../outputs/linkedin/03-antarctica.png">3 · Antarctica</a><a href="../outputs/linkedin/04-europe.png">4 · Europe</a><a href="../outputs/linkedin/05-north-america.png">5 · North America</a><a href="../outputs/linkedin/06-africa-over-asia.png">6 · Africa over Asia</a></nav><h2>Area and shape tell different stories</h2><p>A smaller area does not guarantee that one intact outline will nest inside another. Australia is fully contained in its saved placement. The others retain visible protrusions: the map reports the measured share inside and beyond the host. It makes no claim that every continent literally fits inside Africa.</p><h2>The comparison, reversed</h2><figure><a href="../outputs/asia-country-comparison.png"><img loading="lazy" src="../outputs/asia-country-comparison.png" alt="Africa’s cream outline over colored Asian countries, with a table of covered country portions."></a><figcaption>93.8% of the moved Africa outline falls within Asia; 6.2% extends beyond it. Country colors give geographic context.</figcaption></figure><p>The table describes portions beneath Africa’s repositioned outline. Split countries refer to their Asian portions. These are not whole-country equivalents.</p><div class="table"><table><thead><tr><th>Country / territory</th><th>Extent</th><th>Covered km²</th><th>Share of source piece</th></tr></thead><tbody>ROWS</tbody></table></div><h2>Geography and method</h2><p>Natural Earth 1:50m supplies the outlines. Each continent uses a locally centered WGS84 equal-area representation, normalized minutely to its ellipsoidal surface area. After preparation, only rotation and translation are used. No display simplification or clipping is applied.</p><p>Africa includes Madagascar and the saved regional island grouping. Australia includes Tasmania, rather than all Oceania. Europe and Asia use an explicit saved division through transcontinental countries. Antarctica includes historical perennial ice shelves, excluding seasonal sea ice. Enclosed interior rings are filled consistently; the measure is polygon surface area, not dry-land area.</p><p>These local projections support an area comparison; moving the shapes does not produce exact geographic relocation on the globe. Generalized boundaries and the historic Antarctic extent limit geographic precision.</p><nav class="links"><a href="../README.md">Complete methods</a><a href="../source-manifest.json">Source manifest</a><a href="../outputs/continent-areas.csv">Continent areas CSV</a><a href="../outputs/asia-country-coverage.csv">Country portions CSV</a><a href="../outputs/individual-validation.csv">Validation CSV</a><a href="../outputs/published-area-checks.csv">Published-area checks</a></nav><footer>Thomas Hagos · Made with Natural Earth (public domain). DejaVu Sans.<br>Code, data and full-resolution exports belong in the author’s repository. The shared MUSA gallery receives a thumbnail and linked Markdown entry.</footer></main></html>'''.replace('REPO',base).replace('ROWS',body)
(P/'index.html').write_text(page)
(R/'docs/portfolio-entry.md').write_text('''# Africa, to Scale
Day 03 / Polygons · 2026 MUSA 30-Day Map Challenge

A six-panel continent comparison centered on Africa. Real outlines are represented at one physical area scale, then rotated and repositioned without resizing. The final panel reverses the perspective, placing Africa over country-colored Asia and quantifying covered country portions.

Natural Earth geometry, WGS84 ellipsoidal areas and locally centered equal-area projections support a reproducible Python workflow. The map distinguishes relative area from literal containment, retaining every measured protrusion. Saved placements, source checksums and numerical audits accompany the high-resolution exports.

[Map](../outputs/africa-to-scale.png) · [Project page](../site/index.html) · [Source repository]('''+base+''')
''')
(R/'docs/linkedin-caption.md').write_text('''Day 3: Polygons — Africa, to Scale.

I wanted to compare Africa with continents instead of the usual country puzzle. Each outline keeps its calculated area as it moves and rotates. The last panel flips the comparison: Africa over Asia, with the countries showing what lies beneath it.

Swipe through the individual panels for the area comparisons. Remote islands stay visible in full-extent context insets, while the main views focus on the larger landmasses.

One thing the geometry makes clear: having a smaller area doesn’t mean an outline will fit neatly inside another. I kept those edges visible.

#30DayMapChallenge #teamMUSA
''')
print('Built local project page, portfolio entry and LinkedIn caption.')
